LoopQwen-MLX project · 26 November 2026 · Technical report
= Abstract
Can a small pretrained decoder answer more questions correctly by applying the same layer more than once? We study Qwen3.5-0.8B on an 18 GiB Apple Silicon machine, comparing each trained system with untouched Base on identical requests and separately testing recurrence under matched training. A three-pass native layer solved 124/128 induction problems across two disjoint panels versus 58/128 for an equally trained one-pass layer. The advantage survived a second training seed and an intervention that swapped the evidence class. Matched arithmetic and entailment studies tied on final-answer accuracy.

A two-pass tool model initialized from the instruction-tuned sibling executed the right function and returned its result on 17/22 public requests versus 0/22 for Base. An equally trained one-pass model solved 18/22. With Qwen’s official tool interface, a new two-pass fit scored 21/22 and the same untouched Base scored 20/22. On real files and packages the registered rubric credited 8/12 native conversations against 2/12. Rescoring ordinary prose with correct file names and numbers gave 10/12 against 9/12 with exactly one correct call. On a cold stream of 50 mixed requests, the released system scored 21 versus 12 in 83.97 versus 234.20 seconds, with slower first tokens and one fewer correct arithmetic answer. A single shared adapter improved mixed success to 74/144 versus 62/144 in 107.60 versus 525.22 seconds. Its paired difference was inconclusive ($p=0.148$), and the 72 fresh human questions favored Base, 50 versus 45.

On a 131,086-token register and its key swap, the recurrent system retrieved both changed values against zero for Base in 167.39 versus 390.86 seconds, including weight conversion. Output ran at 29.27 versus 27.42 tokens/s and peak allocation was 3.32 versus 3.78 GiB. An offline release initially lost both historical 131K answers because its evidence mask skipped packed caches. A corrected export recovered both, matched its qualified 24K, book, short-language and live-tool outputs, and improved decoding speed over the earlier export. Both systems missed the human-authored question on a complete 162K-token novel and the actual file size in two complete Git listings of 169,890 and 173,151 tokens. The clearest causal depth gain concerns induction. Practical tool and long-context gains depend on additional choices in supervision, initialization, routing and kernels. The accompanying archives include prompts, emitted tokens, external observations, timing, memory and decisions for each comparison.

On complete real files, a new learned attention reader followed a changed Git object size at 173,129 and 191,099 tokens. It also reported the correct lockfile size in prose. The strict count was 2/3 versus 0/3 and the numerical content count was 3/3 versus 0/3, in 295 versus 811 cold seconds. A separate recurrent tool reviewer distinguished correct from wrong calculator receipts on 16/24 held-out questions versus 2/24, but answered fewer questions correctly in free arithmetic dialogue. A question-contrast model grounded 12/24 new arithmetic answers versus nine while losing on fresh passage reasoning, 2/48 versus 13/48. A semantic audit reduced its apparent lead on real two-request services from 9/12 versus 2/12 to 11/12 versus 9/12.

