= Offline deployment qualification
The tool release contains two text backbones, a shared tokenizer and the local runtime in 2.207 GiB of files. The previous language-only directory occupied 1.443 GiB. An explicit tool list selects the recurrent instruction-initialized tool model. For ordinary requests the wrapper releases the tool backbone and loads the qualified language system. The caller supplies each callback. The export contains no training data lookup or network service used for answering these requests.

The packaged tool branch reproduced all 22 public trajectories, including every input ID, emitted ID, call and observation. We also asked for the installed versions of MLX, NumPy, psutil, safetensors and PyYAML using live Python distribution metadata. Four of five answers contained the correct queried version. For psutil the model returned an incomplete statement without a version. An isolated interpreter with only the bundle and installed dependencies reproduced the first field trace in offline mode.

#figure(image("tool-latency.svg", width: 85%), caption: [Each point is one human-authored numerical request with its actual callbacks. Points below the dashed line finished earlier with the tool model. The symbol records whether the recurrent system completed the correct execution.])

Across the 22 numerical sessions the median time to a terminal outcome fell from 4.197 s to 0.614 s. The nearest-rank 90th percentiles were 5.602 and 1.016 s. Median first-turn time to the first token was slightly higher for the recurrent system (92.81 ms versus 91.46 ms). Aggregate generated tokens per session-second were 71.57 versus 68.02, including repeated prefill and callback time. The endpoint speed advantage comes primarily from shorter conversations.

A balanced crossover on these exact typed weights ran every numerical request four times. The two full-prefill measurements per request totaled 30.14 s across the panel; prefix reuse totaled 27.85 s. All 88 trajectories retained identical generated tokens, function arguments and actual observations. Reuse saved session time on 19 of 22 requests. The largest per-request slowdown was 1.7%. The checkpoint-specific policy reuses 6,656 token positions across the measured sessions.

After switching from tool use, 64 induction, 56 deduction and 80 mixed language generations matched the previous qualified export in full. All 120 raw prose requests used the ordinary path and all five domain losses matched, including pooled NLL 2.982658. Both complete 131,083-token requests reproduced the correct answer, exact token stream and located evidence span. The pair took 525.82 seconds at a peak of 4.33 GiB. This end-to-end test ties context capacity, ordinary language retention and executable tools to the same export inventory.

On the cold 50-request field stream, the released assistant returned the first token sooner on 4/50 requests and reached the end of the request sooner on 38/50. The median wait for a first token was 0.122 s versus 0.089 s for Base. The 95th percentiles were 1.097 s versus 0.522 s. Median time to the end was 1.08 s versus 5.59 s. These distributions include requests that exhausted their budget. Finishing an unsuccessful interaction quickly also contributes to the observed speed.

#figure(image("service-latency.svg",width: 96%),caption: [User wait on each enrolled request, including the initial cold load and switches where they occur. Each curve gives the percentage of requests served within the time on the horizontal axis. The supplement reports paired faster-request counts and nearest-rank upper quantiles.])

== Correct answers within a user’s time budget
For a wall-time threshold we count requests that ended with a correct answer before that time. Every enrolled request stays in the denominator, including invalid calls and exhausted token allowances. On the 72 fresh human questions, the unified model supplied 42 correct completed replies within one second, against zero for Base. At two seconds the counts were 45 and 18. Base overtook the candidate at four seconds (46 versus 45) and ended at 50 versus 45. On all 144 interleaved requests the two-second counts were 74 and 22, reaching 74 and 62 by eight seconds. The different ordering on fresh questions illustrates how the workload and the time available to a user change the observed advantage. These are descriptive thresholds applied to the originally measured runs with fixed token caps.

#figure(image("correct-by-deadline.svg",width:94%),caption:[Fraction of all requests that reached a completed, correct endpoint by elapsed wall time. Each panel uses that study’s own success criterion. The last panel includes only the 72 human questions from the unified comparison.])

== Reusing the tokenizer across live mode switches

Four fresh processes each served the same 50 requests, with policies in ABBA order. Each process held one active backbone at a time. The reuse policy checks tokenizer-defining files before reusing the BPE object. It rebuilt two tokenizers over its two streams, compared with 32 over the two reload streams. All 200 generated trajectories, actual callbacks and terminal outcomes reproduced the frozen field reference. Across 30 switches per policy, first-token time fell 44.3%, from 29.43 to 16.39 s. Summed first-token time over all requests was 26.39 versus 39.45 s; wall time was 152.03 versus 164.20 s. Peak MLX bytes matched at 1,810,571,734. Whole-process maximum RSS was 1,916,698,624 versus 2,701,754,368 bytes. The two policies ran on the same packed release. The installed runtime enables reuse for the exact qualified inventory. The original self-contained archive remains identifiable by its published hash.

#figure(image("tokenizer-streams.svg",width:94%),caption:[Each point measures one entire 50-request stream in a fresh process. Switch latency includes all work from the incoming request to its first emitted token.])

A second crossover on the corrected installed loader and release 04 reproduced all 200 trajectories. Across 30 mode switches per policy, tokenizer reuse cut cumulative wait for the first token from 29.58 to 16.31 s (44.9%). Across all requests it fell from 39.57 to 26.34 s. Total wall time fell from 164.66 to 151.60 s, maximum process RSS from 2.52 to 1.79 GiB, and both policies peaked at 1,810,571,734 native bytes. The installed assistant enables this optimization for both tested inventories. These timings compare two implementations of the same release. One sentence of the replication protocol retained the older release number 03. The registered checkpoint path and the inventory verified by the runner identify release 04.

#figure(image("corrected-tokenizer-streams.svg",width:94%),caption:[Complete release 04 streams in execution order. Each process handled 50 interleaved user requests.])

== Tokenization of user text across writing systems
Transformers uses the custom export model type and tokenizer metadata to choose a tokenizer class. For the offline bundle, the original tokenizer configuration inherited the name Qwen2Tokenizer even though pinned Base uses Qwen3.5Tokenizer. Their word-splitting expressions differ in the treatment of Unicode combining marks. We test whether explicitly naming the Qwen3.5 class in the delivered configuration alone corrects the mismatch. Every learned weight, vocabulary entry, BPE merge and line of bundled runtime code is held fixed.

We selected all 5,587 primary localized territory names in nineteen language files from Unicode Common Locale Data Repository release 47 [54], excluding alternate names and inheritance markers. The 5,587 locale–territory rows cover 295 territory identifiers and 5,364 distinct strings. In 2,942 rows the original spelling contains a character with a Unicode mark category. The independently loaded Base tokenizer supplies the exact reference IDs for each original spelling. Four isolated offline processes re-run the two original full-length register lookups, shorter register requests, two entire books, twelve short requests and two live package calls. Each process then tokenizes all territory names and generates an answer to the original multilingual BoolQ passage. This measures whether users' text reaches the trained model in its intended representation.

#figure(image("localized-inputs.svg",width:97%),caption:[Token IDs compared against ordinary Base for 5,587 localized territory names. Each input is counted once. Two isolated loads per version reproduced every token sequence.])

Release 05 agreed with Base on all 5,587 inputs. Release 04 disagreed on 2,150, distributed over ten locales, all on strings containing a combining mark. The corrected inputs occupied 32,152 tokens compared with 36,584 previously, a reduction of 12.1%. Both versions correctly answered the single multilingual BoolQ question. Release 05 retained both 131,084-token outputs, both 24,593-token outputs, all 512 book next-token decisions and losses, twelve short outputs and two live package sessions. The two streams together took 286.30 versus 286.58 seconds. Peak register allocations were 2,525,708,284 and 2,525,694,460 bytes respectively.

We also asked the installed release in Hindi for the local NumPy version and then for the MLX version. The assistant executed the dependency and version functions on each request. It correctly reported NumPy 2.5.5, received MLX 0.32.4, and answered 2.5.5 again. The two requests took 1.70 and 1.06 seconds. Base printed proposed calls on both requests. The isolated release reproduced both recurrent exchanges. Correct input encoding and access to the current observation still left a measurable error in using that observation.


== Folding adjacent native layers into one mixed-use backbone

The released assistant changes backbones when a caller switches between tools and ordinary language. Bae et al. [38] initialized recursive models from unshared transformers and used depth-specific adapters to recover specialization. We test a local conversion of the qualified instruction sibling. We discard gated-delta layer 21 and run the same physical layer 20 twice, maintaining two convolution and delta states. A rank-192 bottleneck sees the second call’s input and residual. It learns to reconstruct the removed block’s output in units of the input RMS. The 23 remaining physical layers supply 24 layer calls for ordinary text and 25 for tools, which also use the existing final-layer tool adapter. Both modes share the same instruction arrays. The fitting targets are native hidden vectors from 512 prose excerpts and 512 distinct tool conversations, using the rightmost at most 256 fitting tokens and 24 sampled positions per document. Evaluation retains the complete inputs. Another 64 documents measure error against skipping the removed transform or repeating its predecessor without repair.

After decoding the actual 1,024 fitting contexts to text and masking quantities, two of 40 fresh human prompts shared any sequence of five words with the fitting material. The maximum shared contiguous run was five words and the largest five-gram Jaccard similarity was 0.0113. This audit concerns the local adaptation corpus. The pretrained checkpoint’s training material cannot be screened in the same way.

The public comparison uses 20 fresh human word problems, 20 fresh human BoolQ questions, all 22 retention calls from the public numerical suite and the 30 live file, database and package requests of the field benchmark. We preserve each initial prompt ID, repeat the same shuffled 92-request stream on untouched ordinary Base and include the initial load, subsequent switches, actual tool observations and terminal outcomes. Any lost previously correct public action within these subsets closes deployment. A matched one-application attribution study and independent long-context qualification require a passing direct result first.

The correction used 590,848 parameters and 2,363,392 bytes in place of 22,927,968 original layer bytes. Both serving views together occupied 799,740,004 unique parameter bytes. On held-out prose, normalized hidden error fell from 0.2070 with uncompensated repetition to 0.0749. On held-out tool dialogue it fell from 0.3146 to 0.0332. The two views each reproduced their incremental top token. These local checks preceded the independent user-level readout.

