= Human-edited reading questions
== Locating evidence in complete project files

The earlier whole-tree comparison provides a concrete retrieval error. In two complete Git listings, the trained register reader selected offsets 10–36 both times. Its fast prefill retained the entire document in all global caches, but the biased region contained neither requested size. We tested a separate reader on an asset never previously queried and on a dependency lockfile. Each question went to the same trained, native recurrent decoder whenever the new reader found a distinctive phrase.

For every physical line, the reader constructs features describing the coverage and inverse document frequency of one-, two- and three-token sequences from the question. Each document supplies its own frequencies, following the principle that terms shared by few passages carry more retrieval information [59]. Lines above 192 tokens are split with 64 tokens of overlap. Seven fitted coefficients rank all candidate intervals. We trained them on evidence locations for 384 human SQuAD questions and 256 uncompressed sizes from a pinned CPython source archive. Twelve paragraphs formed each SQuAD training context. Metadata training presented up to 40 neighboring package entries. Optimizing the marginal probability of a complete reference span allowed any original window containing the full answer to receive credit. Training and field inputs came from disjoint documents.

At serving time the last question paragraph is separated from the source. A highly localized bigram or trigram in the winning interval activates the reader. It adds attention bias over that interval in the original history. The decoder keeps every input token and generates its own answer without a tool or a second prompt. The packed register prefill still approximates attention by selecting pages. We record the number of attended and possible token pairs, all physical offsets, the actual selected span, and the exact gold span. Capacity, evidence localization and final answer accuracy are three separately observable outcomes.

