== An entire natural novel
We tested the offline assistant on Frank Norris's _McTeague_ using a human-written NarrativeQA question about who reports McTeague's missing dental license to City Hall [19]. Both publisher annotators named Marcus. In the second version we changed all 440 occurrences of Marcus, 24 of Mark and 65 of Schouler to Felix, Ned and Radford respectively. We kept the question and every chapter. The two prompts contained 162,714 and 162,711 tokens.

The release selected its ordinary-depth route on both books. On the original, both systems exhausted all 384 output tokens without supplying a visible answer. On the renamed text both completed a 195-token generation identifying the sheriff. Neither supplied the correct name on either version. All output tokens matched between systems, and all six attention sites retained 163,097 and 162,905 consumed positions respectively. Aggregate first-token times were 571.28 and 571.31 s, full request times 596.38 and 596.42 s, and peaks 4,658,671,388 and 4,653,289,216 bytes. This cleanly separates demonstrated full-history capacity from the ability to answer a human question about it. It closes the claim of a narrative-comprehension gain for the shipped router on this intervention.

= Causal raw-language prediction
#include "raw-results.typ"

*A longer prediction horizon on the first pass.* Multi-token pretraining has used separate heads to predict future tokens from a shared trunk [47]. Here both applications of native layer 23 share their rank-48 Q/K/V/O adapters. During training a width-192 bottleneck on the first application predicts the token two positions ahead. A gated second application predicts the immediate next token with the tied vocabulary, observed text and two frozen model distributions. Both losses update the same block. During inference the auxiliary bottleneck is absent and the model receives only preceding tokens. Adaptation starts from the original 0.8B Base. The two teacher distributions were recorded on the same 2,048 fitting passages.

The independent test uses 128 passages from 24 sources, comprising four novels, four memoirs, eight Git manuals and eight encyclopedia articles. The enrollment excludes shared 24-token windows with the fit and earlier raw test suites. Both passage and original source are units of analysis for the paired sign test. Two historical suites add 172 passages. Each of six free eight-token generations runs incrementally and from its complete prefixes, with both native final-layer caches inspected. This comparison measures causal text prediction.


The pooled comparison favored the two-pass decoder by 73 correct next tokens on 36,991 predictions. It used 21.20 versus 24.27 s and 1.18 versus 1.84 GiB peak allocation. On fresh text alone it made 5,494 versus 5,526 correct decisions over 14,351 positions. Its mean negative log-likelihood increased from 3.27265 to 3.29071. Only 31/128 passages and 2/24 distinct works favored the model on loss. The two-sided sign probabilities were $4.16 times 10^(-9)$ and $3.59 times 10^(-5)$ respectively, in favor of Base. Each of the four new domains lost top-one accuracy. WikiText and the earlier retention panel accounted for the pooled improvement, with 72 and 33 additional correct predictions respectively.

#figure(image("future-token-sources.svg",width:93%),caption:[Each point is one of the 24 fresh works. Negative likelihood changes and positive changes in correct predictions favor the recurrent decoder. Source-level results use all enrolled positions.])

Cached and fresh computation produced the same eight freely generated tokens on each of six independent prefixes with seven full attention histories. A separate installed CLI test generated a 48-token continuation about a library custodian. It repeatedly transferred a box between people. The archived output gives an example of the model’s free prose. The fresh-source result closed promotion.


*Learning a correction across distinct works.* The two-horizon training mixture included 512 documentation windows from 23 Python manuals. Our next training mixture limits each work’s contribution. Its 3,072 windows come from 64 PG-19 training books [51], 128 CPython manuals and 128 Wikipedia articles. Each of the three domains contributes 1,024 windows. Twenty-four additional works supply 96 test windows. An exact 24-token substring screen excludes duplicated passages across partitions and prior raw evaluations. The source mix is small enough to audit at the level of each book or manual. Dolma documents the use and curation of diverse sources at pretraining scale [52].

Starting again from pinned Base, we freeze the ordinary first application, reapply the same BF16 layer with rank-32 attention adapters, and project the difference of normalized states through a learned 64-channel bottleneck. A scalar gate and the ordinary state’s RMS scale the return to the residual stream. The final projection starts at zero. The training objective uses new supervision from the two frozen native teachers and actually observed next tokens. This study changes both the corpus and how the repeated layer affects the ordinary state. Its primary comparison with untouched Base measures their joint effect. Work-specific results and 128 separate prior-work retention windows are prespecified.

The 492,545 trainable parameters received 226,800 target exposures. The 2B and Base teachers each scored 98,304 positions, taking 403.63 and 203.35 seconds. Preparing the frozen prefix took 121.66 s and optimization took 207.81 s. All four original attention projections were shared. On the fixed 32-token probe the maximum change to the ordinary hidden state was exactly zero at initialization and 0.001953125 after fitting. The mean learned gate fell from 0.25 to $2.76 times 10^(-6)$. Across six independent live positions the largest gate was $1.43 times 10^(-5)$. The trained model strongly attenuated the second-pass contribution at these positions.

On 12,288 targets from 24 new works, the model made 5,499 correct next-token decisions against 5,507 for Base. Mean NLL was 2.866857 versus 2.865120. Loss improved on 41 passages and worsened on 55 (two-sided sign $p=0.184$). Aggregating by work, six improved and eighteen worsened ($p=0.0227$, favoring Base). The fresh books lost ten correct decisions, encyclopedia articles lost five and Python manuals gained seven. Across all 49,279 targets the model gained 35 correct decisions while mean NLL increased from 3.087127 to 3.088604. The four scoring suites took 27.55 versus 31.36 s with peaks of 1.18 versus 1.84 GiB. All six cached continuations reproduced the full computation. The registered quality gate closed promotion. The cost comparison includes the eight-bit prefix.

